          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 7 — Particulate Nature of Matter"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.892,
            0.03,
            0.918,
            0.045
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "99"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.083,
            0.743,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You might have collected pebbles and stones from the sand while playing on a riverbank or a beach. Where do these pebbles, stones, and sand come from?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.141,
            0.744,
            0.234
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the mountains, rocks gradually break down due to erosion. Rivers flowing through these regions carry along the eroded rock pieces. As the rivers flow, they continue to break down the rocks further into pebbles, stones, sand; and transport large quantities of them to the plains."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.235,
            0.744,
            0.311
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The bigger rocks are eventually broken down into finer grains of sand and clay. Is this grain the smallest unit of a bigger rock or can these grains of sand and clay be broken down further? Let us find out!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.328,
            0.595,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7.1 What Is Matter Composed of?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.394,
            0.481,
            0.419
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 7.1: Let us explore"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.433,
            0.671,
            0.473
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Take a stick of chalk (Fig. 7.1a) and break it into two pieces (Fig. 7.1b)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.476,
            0.67,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Continue breaking the chalk till it becomes difficult to break it further by hand."
            }
          ]
        },
